<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalervo Oberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalervo Oberg was a Canadian anthropologist, best known for coining the term &quot;culture shock&quot;. His fieldwork in Ankole in the 1930s produced the chapter on the kingdom in the influential collection African Political Systems (1940), which presented Ankole as a state built on the domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahima">Bahima</a> herders over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairu">Bairu</a> cultivators. Later scholars have qualified this interpretation, but it made Ankole a classic case in political anthropology.</p></div>
